"""
J0607-2152 -- HST/WFC3-IR F160W.

Second reduction, replacing the earlier 0.128249"/pix, 3.847" version that
previously lived in this module.

  pixel scale   0.0975059926 arcsec/pix   (frame rotated 78.5 deg, sky flip)
  window        5.0703116170 arcsec (52 x 52 pix)
  units         e/s   (ab_zp = 25.930521)
  exposure      2396.928712 s total  ( = 4 x 599.232178 s )
  origin        the deflector, which sits exactly at the centre of the array
                and therefore at (0, 0).  x/y_image are lens-centred as
                elsewhere in samana.  The source HDF5 used a different origin,
                with coor_lens_center = (-0.018634, 0.153048).

psf_model is the final reconstructed PSF (49 x 49, supersampling 1, FWHM 2.22
px = 0.217"), used at full extent -- no trimming.  It is broader than the
psf_base kernel it replaces: 14% rms change in the wings, and 4.03% of its
flux lies beyond r = 19.5 px, against 1.84% for the base.  Cutting it back to
fit a smaller image would therefore discard real reconstructed signal, so the
cutout was enlarged instead.

The delivered array was 60 x 60 and placed the deflector at pixel (31.5, 29.5),
2 px right of centre.  The cut to 52 x 52 is ASYMMETRIC -- 6 columns off the
left, 2 off the right, 4 rows off the bottom and 4 off the top -- so that
the deflector lands on the array centre.  This matters because
_J0607.likelihood_masks builds its circular mask on
linspace(-window/2, window/2, npix), which assumes the deflector is centred.
52 x 52 is the smallest centred cutout that exceeds the 49 px kernel; 56 x 56
(5.460") is the largest the 60 x 60 source allows and gives more convolution
margin, at 16% more pixels.

CAVEAT: at 49 px the kernel is nearly as wide as the 52 px field, so every
pixel lies within a kernel half-width of an edge and the convolution is
truncated everywhere.  That is inherent to a 4.78" kernel on a 5.07" cutout,
not something the window size can fix.

The furthest quasar image sits at r = 0.931" and the satellite at r = 1.204",
both comfortably inside the window/2.5 = 2.028" cut applied to the imaging
weights.

image ordering is preserved from the earlier J0607 entries, so flux ratios and
keep_flux_ratio_index keep their meaning.

EXPOSURE MAP: the source HDF5 stores a weight map with a modal value of
1381.932, which is NOT an exposure time at this pixel scale -- it is still tied
to the coarser 0.128249"/pix grid.  2396.928712 / 1381.932 = 1.734477, matching
the pixel area ratio (0.128249/0.0975060)**2 = 1.729997 to 0.26%, and only the
larger number factors cleanly (4 x 599.232178 s, a standard WFC3/IR stack).
The map exported here has been multiplied by 1.734477 so that its modal value is
the true total exposure, preserving the spatial structure (low-exposure floor
now 1310 s in this footprint).

noise_map = sqrt(background_rms**2 + max(image_data, 0) / exposure_map) using
that rescaled map.  background_rms and exposure_time are also exported if you
would rather let lenstronomy build the Poisson term from scalars:
J0607_HSTF160W(use_noise_map=False).

psf_variance_map: still derived from the psf_base error map, which the source
HDF5 stores as a RELATIVE variance, (delta_k / k)**2, whereas
lenstronomy/samana expect absolute kernel**2 units.  Passing it unscaled adds
10-2500x kernel**2 of variance at the image positions, which silently removes
the point sources from the likelihood.  It is exported here multiplied by the
NEW psf_model**2.  Note this is the uncertainty of the base PSF, not of the
reconstruction, so it should be conservative; replace it if your colleague can
supply an error map for the final PSF.  The unscaled array is kept as
psf_error_map_relative.
"""
